#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111834 :tutorial/content "When two line segments have the same length, they are **congruent** segments. To indicate that the line segments $\\overline{AB}$ and $\\overline{CD}$ are congruent we write \n\n$$\n\\overline{AB} \\:\\:{\\color{red}\\boldsymbol{\\cong}}\\:\\: \\overline{CD},\n$$\n\nand we use special marks like in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/6d/6d7fd26fce7dce67fa57769db3e96be503051be5c7d2c7714ed48927547d5165.png)\n\nWe can also write \n\n$$\nAB \\:{\\color{blue}=}\\: CD,\n$$\n\nwhich means that the measure of $\\overline{AB}$ is equal to the measure of $\\overline{CD}.$\n\n**Watch out!** The equation \n\n$$\n\\overline{AB} = \\overline{CD}\n$$\n\nis only true when both $\\overline{AB}$ and $\\overline{CD}$ correspond to the same exact segment. When the segments are distinct, we should use the symbol $\\cong$ to represent that the two segments are separate but that they have the same length." :tutorial/id #uuid "a565176c-749f-56b7-aa7c-4d200187c50f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2164 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
